.TH GIT 1
.SH NAME
git \- version control, in git's own formats
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git
.RB [ \-C
.IR dir ]
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git
is git written for SIEOS: not a port, but the same formats, so a
repository made on SIEOS is an ordinary git repository for another
machine's git, and one cloned from elsewhere works here. The desktop has a
Git window on top of it.
.PP
The shell splits words and drops quotes, so everything after
.B \-m
is the message:
.B git commit \-m Fix the parser
(the same for
.BR "git config user.name Alice Example" ).
.PP
Revisions:
.BR HEAD ,
a branch, a tag, a remote's branch
.RB ( origin/main ),
a hash or its first 4 or more digits, then
.BI ~ n
and
.B ^
or
.BR ^2 .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR init " [\fIdir\fP]"
A new, empty repository.
.TP
.BI clone " url " \fR[\fIdir\fR]
Copy a repository, and check out its branch.
.TP
.BR status " [" \-s | \-\-short | \-\-porcelain "] [" \-b | \-\-branch ]
What changed: staged, not staged, untracked.
.B \-s
the short form;
.B \-b
with the branch.
.TP
.BI add " path" "\fR... | " \-A
Stage files
.RB ( \-A " or " \-\-all :
every change).
.TP
.BR rm " [" \-\-cached "] \fIpath\fP..."
Remove files (with
.BR \-\-cached ,
only from the index).
.TP
.BR reset " [\fIpath\fP...]"
Unstage; only the index is reset
.RB ( \-\-hard " and " \-\-soft
are refused).
.TP
.BR restore " [" \-\-staged "] \fIpath\fP..."
The files as the index has them (with
.BR \-\-staged ,
the index as HEAD has it).
.TP
.BR commit " [" \-a | \-\-all "] " \-m " \fImessage\fP"
Commit the index
.RB ( \-a :
every tracked file's changes first;
.B \-am
both).
.TP
.BR log " [" \-\-oneline "] [" \-n " \fIn\fP] [\fIrev\fP]"
The history.
.TP
.BR show " [\fIrev\fP]"
A commit and its changes.
.TP
.BR diff " [" \-\-cached | \-\-staged "] [\fIpath\fP...]"
The files against the index (with
.BR \-\-cached ,
the index against HEAD).
.TP
.BR branch " [" \-a " | " \-d " \fIname\fP | \fIname\fP [\fIrev\fP]]"
List the branches
.RB ( \-a :
the remotes' too), delete one
.RB ( \-d " or " \-D ),
or make one.
.TP
.BR checkout " [" \-b " \fInew\fP] \fIbranch\fP | " "checkout \-\-" " \fIpath\fP..."
Go to a branch (with
.BR \-b ,
a new one); none of the tracked files may have uncommitted changes. With
.BR \-\- ,
take files back from the index.
.TP
.BR switch " [" \-c " \fInew\fP] \fIbranch\fP"
The same as
.BR checkout .
.TP
.BR fetch " [\fIremote\fP]"
Get a remote's new commits.
.TP
.BR pull " [\fIremote\fP [\fIbranch\fP]]"
Fetch, then fast-forward, or make a merge commit when no file changed on
both sides; otherwise stop before changing anything and list the files
("CONFLICT: x changed on both sides").
.TP
.BR push " [" \-u | \-\-set\-upstream "] [\fIremote\fP [\fIbranch\fP]]"
Send commits; only fast-forwards.
.B \-u
remembers the branch's upstream.
.TP
.BR remote " [" \-v "] | " "remote add" " \fIname url\fP"
List the remotes, or add one.
.TP
.BR config " [" \-\-global "] \fIkey\fP [\fIvalue\fP]"
Show or set a setting in
.I .git/config
(or
.IR ~/.gitconfig ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-C " dir"
Work in
.I dir
(first, before the command).
.SH URLS
.BR git:// \fIhost\fP[: \fIport\fP] / \fIpath\fP
(port 9418), and
.BR http(s):// [\fIuser\fP[: \fItoken\fP] @ ] \fIhost\fP / \fIpath\fP
(smart HTTP over TLS, the server's certificate checked). HTTP credentials
are asked only when the server answers 401: from the URL, else
.B credential.username
and
.B credential.token
in the configuration, else on the terminal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .git/
The repository: objects, packs, refs, index, config (version 2 of git's
formats).
.TP
.I ~/.gitconfig
Your settings
.RB ( user.name ,
.BR user.email ;
without them, the account's full name and
.IR name @ hostname ).
.TP
.I .gitignore \fRand\fP .git/info/exclude
Ignored files: globs with
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
.BR ** ;
no
.BR ! .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on an error, 128 outside a repository.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
git clone https://example.org/project.git
git add \-A
git commit \-m Fix the parser
git push
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR make (1),
.BR cc (1)
.SH BUGS
No merges inside one file (conflict markers), rebase, stash, tags made
here, submodules, shallow clones, SSH transport, protocol v2, dumb HTTP,
diff between two commits, SHA-256 repositories, or delta compression of
what is pushed. Packs are read whole into memory: fine for projects of a
few tens of MB.
